<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Zemun</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Taurunum</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Zemun is a district of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgrade">Belgrade</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a>, on the right bank of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a> near its confluence with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sava">Sava</a>. Originally the Celtic stronghold of Taurunum, held by the Scordisci, it became a Roman fort and naval station on the Danube frontier. For much of the 18th and 19th centuries it was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a> border town facing Ottoman Belgrade, and it was joined to Belgrade in 1934.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/zemun.jpg" alt="Zemun" width="320" height="118" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Petar Milošević · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Zemun_panorama_from_Gardo%C5%A1_tower.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Zemun lies on the right bank of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a>, upstream from downtown <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgrade">Belgrade</a> and north of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sava">Sava</a>, in the eastern part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syrmia">Syrmia</a> region. The municipality covers 153 square kilometres and borders <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vojvodina">Vojvodina</a> to the west. Its core is made up of the neighbourhoods of Donji Grad, Gardoš, Ćukovac and Gornji Grad, and to the south it continues into New Belgrade as one built-up area.</p><p>Beneath the loess of Gardoš, Muhar, Ćukovac and Kalvarija run the lagums, artificial underground corridors. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> began digging them at least 1,700 years ago, mostly as food stores, and later they served for supply, hiding and evacuation. Loess is strong and easy to dig, but turns to sand when mixed with water, and this is one of the most active landslide areas in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgrade">Belgrade</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The area has been inhabited since the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neolithic">Neolithic</a> period. The first Celtic settlements date from the 3rd century BCE, when the Scordisci founded both Taurunum and Singidunum across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sava">Sava</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> came in the 1st century BCE, and Taurunum became part of the province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pannonia">Pannonia</a> around 15 CE, with a fortress and a harbour for the fleet of Singidunum. Later the area passed to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantine Empire</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gepids">Gepids</a> and the Bulgarian Empire. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-hungary">Kingdom of Hungary</a> conquered the town in the 12th century, and in the 15th century it was given to the Serbian despot <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/durad-brankovic">Đurađ Branković</a>.</p><p>After the Serbian Despotate fell to Ottoman rule in 1459, Zemun became a military outpost. In 1521 Hungarian forces, including 500 river troops led by Marko Skoblić, resisted Suleyman, but Zemun fell on 12 July and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgrade">Belgrade</a> soon after. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-passarowitz">Treaty of Passarowitz</a> of 1718 gave the town to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a> rule, who organised the Military Frontier there in 1746 and gave Zemun the rights of a military commune in 1749. In 1777 it had 6,800 residents, half <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbs">Serbs</a> and half Germans, Hungarians and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a>. It returned to Serbian control on 5 November 1918 and was absorbed into Belgrade on 1 April 1934.</p><p>Between 1941 and 1944 German forces occupied Zemun as part of the East Syrmia Occupation Zone. The Sajmište concentration camp was set up there, and over 20,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romani-people">Romani</a> and opponents of the Nazi regime died in it.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Zemun is a municipality of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgrade">Belgrade</a>, with 177,908 inhabitants at the 2022 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a>. The White Bear Tavern in Ćukovac, first mentioned in 1658, is the oldest surviving building on the urban territory of Belgrade apart from the Belgrade Fortress. Zemun had no bridge over the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a> until the Pupin Bridge to Borča was completed in 2014.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Zemun” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>There they merged with local Illyrian and Thracian peoples and formed the Scordisci, with strongholds at Singidunum (Belgrade) and <b>Taurunum</b> (Zemun).</blockquote><a href="/state/scordisci">Scordisci</a></li></ul></section>
